{"request_id": "quality_real_full_A-q12", "scenario": "quality_real_full_A", "send_offset_s": 182.835, "outcome": "answered", "tier": "full", "ttft_ms": 1262.5, "total_ms": 1262.5, "waiting_ms": 0.1, "working_ms": 1259.0, "retrying_ms": 0.0, "n_429": 0, "retries": 0, "llm_calls": 3, "tokens_in": 2103, "tokens_out": 231, "queue_len_at_admit": 1, "error": "", "question": "By what factor does H-Net improve data efficiency on DNA modelling compared with isotropic models?", "answer": "H-Net improves data efficiency on DNA modeling by a factor of 3.6 compared to isotropic models [Dynamic Chunking for End-to-End Hierarchical Sequence Modeling, p.4]. This improvement is consistently reported, with each H-Net model achieving the same predecay perplexity as the corresponding isotropic model using approximately 3.6× less data [Dynamic Chunking for End-to-End Hierarchical Sequence Modeling, p.17]. Additionally, H-Net (1-stage) with an M3T1 encoder achieves 3.6× the data efficiency of an isotropic architecture [Dynamic Chunking for End-to-End Hierarchical Sequence Modeling, p.37]. While one section mentions a \"nearly 4× improvement\" in data efficiency over baselines for languages and modalities with weaker tokenization heuristics including DNA [Dynamic Chunking for End-to-End Hierarchical Sequence Modeling, p.1], the specific factor cited for DNA modeling compared to isotropic models is 3.6 [S1, S3, S4].", "meta": {"send_offset_s": 182.83502220000082, "qid": "q12", "tier": "full", "forced": true, "scope": "out", "retrieval_conf": 0.614, "verify_log": [{"verdict": "PASS", "issues": []}], "answer_outcome": "answered"}, "spans": [{"kind": "wait", "name": "admission", "start_ms": 0.1, "end_ms": 0.2}, {"kind": "wait", "name": "slot", "start_ms": 0.2, "end_ms": 0.2}, {"kind": "work", "name": "understand", "start_ms": 0.2, "end_ms": 398.9}, {"kind": "work", "name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 399.0, "end_ms": 399.8}, {"kind": "wait", "name": "slot", "start_ms": 399.9, "end_ms": 399.9}, {